# Evidence-First Project Execution

- Before choosing work, load the compact handoff bootstrap. Read a long-term plan,
  milestone, report, or log only when the handoff points to its exact relevant
  section or a material conflict requires expansion.
- Treat user instructions, repository files, Git state, and reproducible command
  output as truth; conversation memory and model summaries are claims to verify.
- Within an approved active milestone, execute the largest cohesive batch that
  shares frozen invariants, scope, and acceptance gates and fits one model context.
  The exact next action is the restart pointer, not a forced handoff boundary.
- Continue through the batch autonomously: implement, test, repair in-scope local
  failures, refactor reversible mechanics, and update owned evidence until all
  batch gates pass or a recorded stop condition triggers.
- Record verified units in the owned plan/report, but do not rewrite status or
  switch models after every micro-step. Hand back at batch completion, a material
  feasibility change, a stop trigger, or context exhaustion.
- Implementation without the contract's tests or evidence is not complete.
- For high-risk behavioral corrections, implement only after failing tests cover
  the invariant matrix. Control timing with deterministic hooks/barriers, never
  sleeps, timestamp luck, or one observed interleaving.
- On a second post-fix failure of the same invariant, stop editing and return for
  high-risk replanning instead of applying another case-specific patch.
- Prefer capable-direct execution when explaining the batch plus expected
  correction loops would cost as much as doing the judgment-heavy work once.
- Continue automatically only inside the recorded scope and authority boundary.
- Stop for material goal, architecture, acceptance, privacy, security, license,
  external-write, destructive-action, or unresolved source-of-truth conflicts.
- Use `.cursor/rules/model-handoff.mdc` whenever responsibility or model changes.
